#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119974 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d87cbd6-7c9b-5f31-871a-b21000755ef0" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Positive Number Squared"}] :question/id #uuid "e16e7b36-a5fe-5ccd-bb4c-008a01afd5cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13013" :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{15^2}\\,$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a\n$$\n\nIn our case, since the number under the square root ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$) is already written as a square, we obtain the answer immediately:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{15^2} = 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119975 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c66352-91eb-5859-a346-0974b3b644ff" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square"}] :question/id #uuid "998557f9-f4d0-594f-ad52-5fa27a73440f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13014" :question/problem "Calculate $\\sqrt{36}$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a\n$$\n\nTo work out $\\sqrt{36}$, we express $36$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{36} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{6 \\cdot 6} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{6^{2}} &=  \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5aec46-708e-5de8-ad56-ce0c2a711fe2" :knowledge-point/title "The Square Root of a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "7684ddd0-1e24-57e4-a486-4cdb021a68bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13015" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}}$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $9$ and $64$ are both perfect squares, where\n\n$$\n9=3^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n64 = 8^2\n$$\n\nSo, to work out\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}}\n$$\n\nwe express\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{64}\n$$\n\nas a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{3^{2}}{8^{2}}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{8 \\cdot 8}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{8})^{2}} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e3ad2c-3335-5bdd-b7ee-8abcfa4c6951" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Square Root Is a Real Number"}] :question/id #uuid "35769a1e-b922-5c68-a4ca-269526a692b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13016" :question/problem "Which of the following square roots are real numbers?\n\n1. $\\sqrt{0}$\n2. $\\sqrt{1.5}$\n3. $\\sqrt{-4}$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a negative number is not a real number, while the square root of a non-negative number is a real number.\n\nLet's look at each square root in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{0}$ is the square root of $0$, which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{0}$ is a real number. In fact, since $0 = 0^2$, we have\n$\\sqrt{0} = \\sqrt{0^2} = 0$.\n- $\\sqrt{1.5}$ is the square root of $1.5$, which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{1.5}$ is a real number.\n- $\\sqrt{-4}$ is the square root of $-4$, which is a negative number. So $\\sqrt{-4}$ is not a real number.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}]}
